# Dependencies.
# RULE: everything required to RUN the product ships in the repo AND the zip —
# no developer or customer ever runs `composer install` to use the plugin.
# So the prod Composer autoloader and every runtime library are TRACKED:
#   - autoload.php + composer/   → the autoloader infrastructure
#   - woocommerce/action-scheduler → runtime `require` (scheduled jobs)
# Dev-only tooling (phpunit, phpstan, wpcs, polyfills, …) stays ignored and is
# restored locally via `composer install`.
#
# Hand-vendored libraries that Composer does NOT manage live in /libs (fully
# tracked, never ignored) — see libs/edd-sl-sdk/WBCOM-PATCHES.md. Keeping them
# under /vendor meant they survived only via negation rules here, so any
# `composer install` that prunes vendor could ship a plugin with a half-present
# SDK.
/vendor/*
!/vendor/autoload.php
!/vendor/composer/
!/vendor/woocommerce/
# NEVER commit this one. `composer install` (with dev) regenerates it listing
# the dev packages' bootstrap files, which the autoloader then eagerly
# requires — and dev packages are not tracked, so a fresh clone fataled on
# every page before the plugin could even print its own "autoloader is
# corrupt" notice. With --no-dev there are no files entries at all, so
# Composer deletes the file and the autoloader never looks for it.
/vendor/composer/autoload_files.php
/node_modules/

# Build artifacts
/build/
/dist/

# IDE
.idea/
.vscode/
*.sublime-*

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Testing
.phpunit.result.cache
/coverage/

# Logs
*.log
debug.log

# Environment
.env
.env.local
__pycache__/
